00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:18,700
If you go and plot the probability

257
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:22,160
that a policy exists, you know, as a function of its popularity,

258
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:25,780
there's a high line for policies that exist.

259
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:28,740
So popular policies that exist almost always last,

260
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:32,280
but popular policies that don't exist often happen,

261
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:33,740
but it can take decades.

262
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:35,700
On the other hand, unpopular policies

263
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:38,020
that exist often last, but it's really unusual

264
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:40,820
for an unpopular policy that doesn't exist to become a law.

265
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:42,200
That's very unusual.

266
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:47,120
Okay. So anyway, so Whitman's bottom line is economists are

267
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:50,700
usually skeptical about stories of collusion in markets.

268
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,580
So shouldn't we be equally skeptical about stories

269
