00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:51,540
somehow teachers have a stranglehold.

315
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:54,060
If there's something that you, so imagine

316
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:56,500
that there's someone who's blocking that door, right,

317
00:12:56,500 --> 00:12:58,380
and they're bigger and stronger than all of us

318
00:12:58,380 --> 00:12:59,180
in this class together.

319
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,120
What is the obvious thing for us to do rather than starve

320
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:04,900
to death in this room while the big person there,

321
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:06,860
well, I mean, we only take our phones.

322
00:13:06,860 --> 00:13:07,760
No one knows where we are.

323
00:13:07,880 --> 00:13:09,660
So obviously you can probably just call 911.

324
00:13:09,660 --> 00:13:12,540
Anyway, so there's a big person.

325
00:13:12,540 --> 00:13:13,800
They won't let us out.

326
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:15,880
Then we have to solve the problem ourselves.

327
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:17,040
We can't physically beat them.

328
00:13:17,580 --> 00:13:19,120
So what is an obvious thing to do?

329
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:20,380
You're economists.

330
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:22,640
Yeah, bribe them.

331
00:13:23,060 --> 00:13:24,860
Say, all right, everyone, like open up the wallets.

332
00:13:24,860 --> 00:13:25,740
Let's take out the money.

333
00:13:25,740 --> 00:13:26,900
What is it going to take to get you

334
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:27,980
to let us leave this room?

335
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:31,940
And like, I'm blocking you.

336
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:34,160
Yes, but what would it take to change your mind?

337
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:35,460
All right.

338
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:38,120
And, of course, you would expect, given the importance

339
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:40,120
of getting out of this room, that we would come

340
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:42,020
up with the money to, come up with the money to get them

341
00:13:42,020 --> 00:13:42,880
to go and leave us alone.

342
00:13:43,300 --> 00:13:46,220
Similarly, if it really were true that merit pay is great

343
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:50,260
for schools and teachers' unions are somehow able to block it,

344
00:13:50,620 --> 00:13:53,320
you think that we would just go and collect a giant bag of money

345
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:57,160
and give it to them and say, now can we have,

346
00:13:57,160 --> 00:13:59,080
so now can we have merit pay, please?

347
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:00,120
Is this enough?

348
00:14:00,220 --> 00:14:01,940
How many, how much money is it going to take

349
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:02,880
to make this problem go away?

350
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:05,840
Right? And by the definition of inefficiency,

351
00:14:06,240 --> 00:14:08,680
as long as the transactions are not important,

352
00:14:08,680 --> 00:14:09,340
this should be doable.

353
00:14:09,340 --> 00:14:13,040
So if we value merit pay at $10 billion

354
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:15,620
and the teachers' union values not having merit pay

355
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:18,960
at $2 billion, we just go and offer them $3 billion

356
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:20,240
and they should be willing to go away.

357
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:21,640
It really is inefficient.

358
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:24,220
Okay. All right.

359
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:26,920
So, and yet it's very common to say

360
00:14:26,920 --> 00:14:28,900
that it's just too hard to buy them off.

361
00:14:29,340 --> 00:14:30,340
Well, why is it too hard?

362
00:14:30,840 --> 00:14:31,800
Well, of course, one would be

363
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:33,820
if actually the status quo is efficient and we just,

364
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:37,720
and if we pay the total value to us of changing policy,

365
00:14:37,720 --> 00:14:39,760
it wouldn't actually compensate the insiders.

366
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:40,620
That's possible.

367
00:14:41,140 --> 00:14:43,060
But again, then there's not really any economic efficiency

368
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:44,260
problem, right?

369
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:45,560
And otherwise, what would the problem be?

370
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:46,560
All right.

371
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:50,860
So this brings us to Whitman's third principle,

372
00:14:50,860 --> 00:14:53,360
which is political bargaining can eliminate any remaining

373
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:54,440
significant inefficiencies.

374
00:14:55,620 --> 00:14:57,420
So yeah, I mean, it's reasonable to say, well, yeah,

375
00:14:57,420 --> 00:14:59,960
if it's like a $5 inefficiency, then congressmen aren't going

376
00:14:59,960 --> 00:15:02,580
to be worried about bargaining over a $5 inefficiency.

377
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:05,820
But anything important, if they're really,

378
00:15:05,820 --> 00:15:08,440
if the world really would be richer with one policy rather

379
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:10,700
than another, we'll just bargain to that equilibrium.

380
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:14,520
Right? And this will affect, as Coase taught us, what?

381
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:15,880
Coase theorem.

382
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:19,100
So the assignment, so if a special interest has power,

383
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:20,560
this will affect what?

384
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:22,580
Distribution.

385
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:26,260
The special interest will be laughing in the money,

386
00:15:26,560 --> 00:15:28,340
doing snow angels in money.

387
00:15:28,340 --> 00:15:29,420
Yes, we're rich.

388
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:30,760
Teachers union.

389
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:31,700
Ah, ha, ha, ha.

390
00:15:31,700 --> 00:15:33,760
We're so full of money.

391
00:15:33,760 --> 00:15:37,120
All right, but this will not affect the other thing,

392
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,460
which is efficiency.

393
00:15:39,460 --> 00:15:41,460
So we will actually do the efficient thing,

394
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:42,560
and we will just buy them off.

395
00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:45,900
Okay. So why does Whitman say this?

396
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:48,320
So first of all, democracy is just designed

397
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:49,600
to have low transactions costs.

398
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:53,340
So first of all, majority rule is cheaper than unanimity.

399
00:15:53,340 --> 00:15:56,300
So when you are building a skyscraper,

400
00:15:56,360 --> 00:15:59,660
50,000 people work together, in general, what percentage

401
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:01,380
of the workers there are there voluntarily?

402
00:16:01,380 --> 00:16:03,260
One hundred percent.

403
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:05,920
It's possible someone's family is being held hostage

404
00:16:05,920 --> 00:16:07,760
to make them do the plumbing or something like that,

405
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:08,760
but that's unusual.

406
00:16:09,180 --> 00:16:11,800
So there you have 100% cooperation,

407
00:16:11,800 --> 00:16:13,620
and that's the rule of markets.

408
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:15,920
Even if a market project is enormous,

409
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:17,400
you still need 100% consent.

410
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:19,660
And what is democracy all about?

411
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:21,380
Not having 100% consent.

412
00:16:22,180 --> 00:16:24,320
Now, of course, there are plenty of possible problems

413
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:25,960
with doing things to people without their consent,

414
00:16:26,980 --> 00:16:29,480
but it does reduce transaction costs.

415
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:32,440
That's undeniable.

416
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:37,920
So if this group could only eat a meal if we have 100%

417
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:39,300
of us agreed on what we were going to eat,

418
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:42,500
we might go hungry, right?

419
00:16:42,680 --> 00:16:45,900
If there's like one vegan and one anti-vegan

420
00:16:45,900 --> 00:16:49,700
who will only eat animal products, then we starve,

421
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:51,420
right, unless, of course, there's bargaining.

422
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:56,520
Okay. So, you know, so, but on the other hand,

423
00:16:56,520 --> 00:16:59,960
with majority rule, we would be able to work out something.

424
00:16:59,960 --> 00:17:02,280
Something would prevail, and we would not starve, right?

425
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:04,420
So there would be lower transaction costs, at least.

426
00:17:04,840 --> 00:17:07,240
And again, in the case of some things,

427
00:17:07,240 --> 00:17:09,180
the transaction costs might be everything.

428
00:17:09,180 --> 00:17:10,260
We might not be able to do it.

429
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:11,200
All right.

430
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:13,380
So majority rule is cheaper than the unanimity rule,

431
00:17:13,380 --> 00:17:15,580
and that's the most obvious argument in favor

432
00:17:15,580 --> 00:17:18,580
of majority rule is just that it saves on bargaining costs.

433
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:21,500
Imagine if the only way for there to be any political change

434
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:22,960
in the U.S. was unanimous consent.

435
00:17:24,340 --> 00:17:24,940
I think it's a fair bet

436
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:26,560
to say there would never be another change again.

437
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:28,400
No legal change anyway.

438
00:17:28,760 --> 00:17:30,460
There could be coup d'etats and things.

439
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:33,140
There could be illegal changes, but we couldn't change the law

440
00:17:33,140 --> 00:17:35,760
because there's no law that everyone would agree to.

441
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:37,780
So it should lead to be something.

442
00:17:38,100 --> 00:17:39,300
Now, remember back in week one,

443
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:40,600
there's weird people in this country.

444
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:42,380
There's weird people in every country.

445
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:43,880
There's misanthropes.

446
00:17:44,180 --> 00:17:46,680
There's people who worship, you know, Kim Jong-un,

447
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:48,660
and there's people like that in this country.

448
00:17:48,660 --> 00:17:51,460
So if there's one person like that,

449
00:17:51,820 --> 00:17:54,300
these are not going to get unanimity, right?

450
00:17:54,300 --> 00:17:56,600
And then furthermore, representative democracy,

451
00:17:57,120 --> 00:17:59,280
and representative democracy is contrasted

452
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:00,560
with what other kind of democracy?

453
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:05,360
Direct democracy also saves transactions costs, right?

454
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:07,700
Because instead of having 300 million Americans bargain,

455
00:18:07,700 --> 00:18:09,980
we just have a few hundred representatives bargain,

456
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:11,340
and so that makes things cheaper.

457
00:18:11,940 --> 00:18:13,180
Same logic holds for committees.

458
00:18:13,180 --> 00:18:14,580
You don't have everyone in Congress bargain.

459
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:18,320
Instead, you just have 16 specialists go and bargain

460
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:20,320
and work something out and hammer it out in a committee,

461
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:23,180
and then they take it to the rest of Congress, right?

462
00:18:23,180 --> 00:18:26,040
And again, the general idea here is that political bargaining,

463
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:28,200
which has a special name, right,

464
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:29,980
at least in the United States,

465
00:18:29,980 --> 00:18:31,820
we call political bargaining log rolling.

466
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:34,880
So as often in American politics,

467
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:36,260
this is an agricultural metaphor,

468
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:40,080
because in farm times, you often need

469
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:43,440
to roll a log off your property, and a log is too big

470
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:44,860
for one farmer to roll.

471
00:18:45,340 --> 00:18:48,260
So how do farmers roll logs off their property?

472
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:51,900
They go over to the other farmer and say, come on, brother,

473
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:53,220
could you help me move this log?

474
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:55,960
He says, well, sure, if you would help me move my logs,

475
00:18:56,340 --> 00:18:59,380
and so this way, things that are too big for any one farmer

476
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:00,140
to move get moved.

477
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:03,200
Similarly, can one congressman pass a law by himself?

478
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:05,420
No. So how do you pass laws?

479
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:07,460
You go and you get friends to help you go

480
00:19:07,460 --> 00:19:09,360
and move the giant logs of legislation,

481
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:11,160
and why do they do this?

482
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:14,260
Because you'll help them when they need help, right?

483
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:18,240
In the old days, the system in the United States used to be,

484
00:19:18,480 --> 00:19:20,880
I'll vote for your bill, and then later on,

485
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:22,920
you'll vote for my bill, right?

486
00:19:23,020 --> 00:19:25,960
But one of the big changes in American politics is

487
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:29,080
that now, instead of having separate bills and trading

488
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:33,100
across the bills, usually people now say, I don't like that plan.

489
00:19:33,420 --> 00:19:35,300
Why don't we just make one really big bill

490
00:19:35,660 --> 00:19:38,840
that has everything in it, and then we don't need

491
00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:40,960
to trust each other anymore, right?

492
00:19:40,960 --> 00:19:42,600
And so that's very, you know,

493
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:44,760
very dangerous in American politics now.

494
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:47,060
Most, you know, a very large piece

495
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:49,820
of legislation will have thousands of pages now.

496
00:19:49,820 --> 00:19:51,680
So I think TARP was over a thousand.

497
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:54,560
I think Affordable Care Act was like 2,500 pages.

498
00:19:55,160 --> 00:19:56,280
It's not all about health care.

499
00:19:56,280 --> 00:20:20,220
It's not all about a relief, not a relief.


